```
============================================================
Detection: Persistence via scheduled task creation
Technique: T1053.005 - Scheduled Task/Job: Scheduled Task
Tactic:    Persistence (TA0003)
Platform:  Splunk SPL. Counterpart to rule.kql (Sentinel) and rule.yml (Sigma base).
Data:      XmlWinEventLog : Security, EventCode 4698 (a scheduled task was created)
Author:    William Richardson   Date: 2026-07-08

SCOPE - what this rule can and cannot see
  Only sees EventCode 4698 (task CREATED). Misses modifications to existing tasks (4702)
  and enable/disable of existing tasks (4700/4701). Cannot see grandchild processes /
  extensions if the task launches a jump script/program.

FIRING LOGIC (weighted score - see SEVERITY block)
  High          = Score >= 4   (two strong signals, or a strong signal plus elevated auto-start)
  Medium        = Score 3      (a strong signal plus one weak, or elevated AND auto-start)
  Informational = Score 2      (a single strong signal: staging path, script ext, LOLBin args,
                                encoded command, or registry-staged/fileless)
  elevation-only / trigger-only (Score 1) never fire on their own.
  Raise the floor to Score >= 3 to drop Informational; raise the High cut to >= 5 if a plain
  two-strong-signal task (e.g. staging path + script ext) is too aggressive as High.

STRONG SIGNALS (weight 2): susp_path, susp_ext, susp_args, susp_encoded, susp_fileless.
WEAK SIGNALS   (weight 1): susp_elevation, susp_trigger (+1 synergy when BOTH are present).

FALSE POSITIVES / TUNING
  - Legit admin/automation using IEX, Invoke-WebRequest, or -EncodedCommand will match.
  - Time/Calendar triggers are deliberately NOT scored (far too common for benign tasks) -
    time-based persistence is caught only via the command-line signals, not the trigger.
  - Missing 4702 correlation - task modifications are a clear blind spot.

TA ASSUMPTION: assumes Splunk_TA_windows auto-extracts the EventData fields (TaskName,
  SubjectUserName, SubjectDomainName). TaskContent is pulled from _raw and decoded here
  regardless. If TaskName/Subject* are empty, the TA isn't extracting - add rex on _raw.
============================================================
```
index=winevents sourcetype=XmlWinEventLog source="XmlWinEventLog:Security" EventCode=4698

``` --- TaskContent = the task-definition XML. Prefer a TA-extracted field, else pull from
    _raw, then entity-decode (&lt; -> <, &gt; -> >, &quot; -> ", &amp; -> &) so tags and
    paths compare as real XML. The decode is a no-op if the TA already decoded it. ```
| rex field=_raw "(?s)Name='TaskContent'>(?<TaskContent_raw>.*?)</Data>"
| eval TaskContent = coalesce(TaskContent, TaskContent_raw)
| eval TaskContent = replace(replace(replace(replace(TaskContent,"&lt;","<"),"&gt;",">"),"&quot;","\""),"&amp;","&")

``` --- who created it (domain\user) --- ```
| eval SubjectAccount = coalesce(SubjectDomainName,"") . "\\" . coalesce(SubjectUserName,"")

``` --- per-action fields (multivalue if the task has >1 <Exec> block); triggers; COM GUID.
```
| rex field=TaskContent max_match=0 "<Command>(?<Command>[^<]*)</Command>"
| rex field=TaskContent max_match=0 "<Arguments>(?<Arguments>[^<]*)</Arguments>"
| rex field=TaskContent max_match=0 "<WorkingDirectory>(?<WorkingDirectory>[^<]*)</WorkingDirectory>"
| rex field=TaskContent max_match=0 "<(?<TriggerTypes>\w+Trigger)>"
| rex field=TaskContent max_match=0 "<ClassId>(?<GUID>[^<]*)</ClassId>"
| eval TriggerTypes = mvjoin(TriggerTypes, ",")

| eval CmdLine = lower(coalesce(mvjoin(Command," "),"") . " " . coalesce(mvjoin(Arguments," "),""))

``` --- SIGNALS ------------------------------------------------------------------------- ```
| eval susp_path      = if(match(TaskContent, "(?i)(Users.Public.|AppData.Local.Temp.|AppData.Roaming.|ProgramData.|Windows.Temp.|PerfLogs.|Downloads.)"), 1, 0)
| eval susp_ext       = if(match(TaskContent, "(?i)\.(bat|cmd|com|hta|js|jse|lnk|ps1|scr|url|vbe|vbs|wsf)</Command>"), 1, 0)
| eval susp_args      = if(match(CmdLine,     "(?i)(-noni|-w hidden|-windowstyle hidden|bypass|frombase64string|iex\(| iex |invoke-expression|downloadstring|downloadfile|mshta|rundll32|regsvr32)"), 1, 0)

| eval susp_encoded   = if(match(CmdLine, "(?i)-e(c|nc|ncodedcommand)?[ ]+[a-z0-9+/=]{12,}"), 1, 0)

| eval susp_elevation = if(match(TaskContent, "(?i)(HighestAvailable|S-1-5-18|<UserId>SYSTEM|<UserId>LocalSystem)"), 1, 0)
| eval susp_trigger   = if(match(TaskContent, "(?i)(<LogonTrigger|<BootTrigger)"), 1, 0)

``` susp_fileless: registry-staged / fileless payload. Fires only when the action BOTH reads a
    value out of the registry AND decodes-or-executes it .```
| eval susp_fileless  = if(match(CmdLine, "(?i)(get-itemproperty|get-itempropertyvalue|reg(\.exe)? query|hkcu:|hklm:|hkey_)")
                          AND match(CmdLine, "(?i)(frombase64string|iex\(| iex |invoke-expression|::getstring|decompress)"), 1, 0)

``` ------------------------------------------------------------
    SEVERITY - weighted score (mirror of the KQL). Five strong signals = 2 each
    (args / ext / path / encoded / fileless), elevation/trigger = 1 each (normal alone for
    admin tasks), +1 synergy when a task both auto-starts AND runs elevated.
    ------------------------------------------------------------ ```
| eval Score = (susp_args*2) + (susp_ext*2) + (susp_path*2) + (susp_encoded*2) + (susp_fileless*2) + (susp_elevation*1) + (susp_trigger*1) + if(susp_elevation=1 AND susp_trigger=1, 1, 0)
| eval Severity = case(Score>=4, "High", Score>=3, "Medium", Score>=2, "Informational", true(), "None")

``` firing floor; raise to >= 3 to drop Informational ```
| where Score >= 2

``` --- DISPLAY NORMALISATION (runs AFTER scoring so it can't skew CmdLine/Score) ---```
| eval Command          = coalesce(Command, if(isnotnull(GUID), "COM:" . GUID, null()))
| eval Command          = if(isnull(Command)          OR Command="",          "(none)", Command)
| eval Arguments        = if(isnull(Arguments)        OR Arguments="",        "(none)", Arguments)
| eval WorkingDirectory = if(isnull(WorkingDirectory) OR WorkingDirectory="", "(none)", WorkingDirectory)

``` TaskContent (full XML) is kept in the table for wider context; drop it for a more readable
    alert, at the cost of losing the actions/triggers/principal at a glance. ```
| table _time, host, SubjectAccount, TaskName, Severity, Score, Command, Arguments, WorkingDirectory, TriggerTypes, TaskContent
| sort - Score, - _time

```
------------------------------------------------------------
ANALYST TRIAGE & FOLLOW-UP
------------------------------------------------------------
Pivot fields from the alert: host, TaskName, GUID, SubjectAccount.

STEP 1 - Triage the creation event itself
  - Is SubjectAccount an expected admin/service account, or a standard user / suspicious account?
  - Does Command/Arguments point at a staging path (Temp, Public, ProgramData, Downloads)?
  - Is the payload an encoded command (-enc <base64>) or pulled from the registry? Decode it:
      powershell -c "[Text.Encoding]::Unicode.GetString([Convert]::FromBase64String('<blob>'))"
  - Does the task auto-start (TriggerTypes = Logon/Boot) AND run elevated (SYSTEM / HighestAvailable)?
    Auto-start + elevated + staging path together is a strong persistence signal.

STEP 2 - Scope: has the task been touched again after creation?
  OPTION A - Correlate task lifecycle events for the same task
             (4699 deleted / 4700 enabled / 4701 disabled / 4702 updated).
             NOTE: run *history* (last run time / result) is NOT in the Security log - use Option B.

    index=winevents sourcetype=XmlWinEventLog source="XmlWinEventLog:Security"
        EventCode IN (4698,4699,4700,4701,4702) host="<host>" TaskName="<TaskName>"
    | table _time, EventCode, TaskName, SubjectUserName, host
    | sort _time

  OPTION B - Live check on the host (RDP or PS remoting) for actual execution history:
    B.1  Get-ScheduledTask -TaskName "<leaf name>" | Get-ScheduledTaskInfo
           -> LastRunTime / LastTaskResult / NextRunTime
    B.2  Get-WinEvent -LogName "Microsoft-Windows-TaskScheduler/Operational" |
           Where-Object { $_.Message -match "<TaskName>" }
           -> per-run start/stop/result history (event IDs 100/102/200/201)

STEP 3 - Pull the full task definition for deeper analysis
    index=winevents sourcetype=XmlWinEventLog source="XmlWinEventLog:Security"
        EventCode=4698 host="<host>" TaskName="<TaskName>"
    | table _time, SubjectUserName, TaskContent   -- full XML: actions, triggers, principal

STEP 4 - Decision
  - Confirmed malicious -> isolate the device, capture the task XML + any binaries it references,
    hunt the same TaskName / Command across other hosts, then begin DFIR procedures.
  - Benign admin/automation -> add SubjectAccount + Command to the tuning allow-list (see FP notes).
------------------------------------------------------------
```